---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="The line">
  <h1>The line</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Three generations are known to exist. One of them has a name. This page will grow, and its present
    emptiness is the most accurate thing on the site.
  </p>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      Two constraints shape this page. <strong>Living people are omitted from the build</strong>, which
      removes the most recent generations; and the generations above them are <strong>not yet
      documented</strong>. What remains publishable today is a skeleton.
      <a href={u("/method")}>Method &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>As it stands</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Gen</th><th>Person</th><th>Dates</th><th>Where</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">1</td>
          <td><strong>Pablo Armando Lerena</strong></td>
          <td class="mono">b. 22 Feb 1882</td>
          <td>Born <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe</strong>, Argentina; living at <strong>Rugby, Cape
            Town</strong> in 1940</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span><br /><span class="small">Argentine passport,
            Série A No. 07962, 24 Dec 1940. Trainer; 6th Cavalry 1915; a widower by 1940.
            Date of death not yet established.</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">1?</td>
          <td><strong>Robert (Roberto) Paul Lerena</strong></td>
          <td class="mono">d. 1950</td>
          <td>Rugby and Camps Bay, Cape Town</td>
          <td><span class="chip">Open</span><br /><span class="small">Built the 1931 stables. May be the
            same man as Pablo Armando — <em>Pablo</em> is <em>Paul</em>, and both lived at Rugby — or a
            close relative. The estate file decides it.</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">2</td>
          <td><strong>Nuno Fernando Lerena</strong><br />
            <span class="small">m. Catherine Mary Sophia <strong>Booyzen</strong>, 1929&ndash;2013</span></td>
          <td class="mono">6 Oct 1924 &ndash; 1 Jul 1971</td>
          <td>South Africa; died in the Transvaal</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span><br /><span class="small">Confirmed twice over — the
            family tree and the deceased-estate file MHG 6746/71, which names the same widow.
            <strong>His descent from generation 1 is not yet proven.</strong></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">3</td>
          <td><strong>Cheryl Anne Lerena</strong></td>
          <td class="mono">b. 1955</td>
          <td>South Africa; later Brisbane</td>
          <td><span class="chip">Omitted</span><br /><span class="small">Married Ivan Defranceski.
            Withheld under the rule on living people.</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small">
    Cheryl's brothers and sisters — Jeanette Marie (1949&ndash;2021), Paul Raymond (1951&ndash;2000),
    Catherine Tersia (1952&ndash;1999) and Patricia Carmen (1958&ndash;2023) — are all deceased and are
    recorded in the project's data. Note <strong>Paul</strong> recurring from generation 1, and
    <strong>Carmen</strong> surviving as a given name into 1958.
  </p>

  <h2>The gap that remains</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Nothing yet joins generation 2 to generation 1.</strong> That Nuno Fernando (b. 1924) is Pablo
    Armando's son is the obvious reading — Pablo was 42 in 1924, both are Cape-connected, and the family
    kept Iberian given names for three generations — but it is a reading, not a document. It is closed by
    Nuno's birth or death record, or by Pablo's estate file.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it joins the other archives</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cheryl Anne Lerena married Ivan Defranceski.</strong> That marriage is the hinge between this
    archive and the <em>Defranceski</em> archive, whose line runs back through Johannesburg to Gračišće and
    the Istrian peninsula. Their son married into the <em>Falco</em> line of Arienzo in Campania.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three archives, three continents of origin, meeting in Brisbane: <strong>Lerena</strong> from the River
    Plate by way of South Africa, <strong>Defranceski</strong> from Istria, <strong>Falco</strong> from
    Campania.
  </p>

  <h2>The first task</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Order the Cape estate file MOOC 6/9/17017.</strong> It settles whether Pablo Armando and Robert
    Paul are one man, and a Cape death notice of that period names the deceased's parents and birthplace —
    which would carry this line a generation further back, into Argentina.
  </p>
  <p><a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
</Base>
